        "consistency_check": "The most plausible value, 0.17, is inside [-0.23, 0.57]. A normal distribution has unbounded signed support and therefore recognizes that removing blitzes can hurt a defense that lacks a viable four-man rush; interval endpoints are approximate because of rounding."
      }
    },
    {
      "bucket": "intuition",
      "type": "game_management",
      "title": "Timeouts as defensive-front recovery tools",
      "recommendation": "Use an occasional first-half timeout during an opponent's tempo drive when the interior front is visibly exhausted, misaligned, or unable to substitute safely, even if the clock-only calculation argues for saving it. Restrict this to credible touchdown-threat situations rather than using timeouts merely to organize the sideline.",
      "rationale": "A timeout can prevent one fatigue-driven explosive play or red-zone breakdown, but public timeout models generally do not observe line fatigue, communication degradation, or substitution quality well enough to establish this rule. This is coaching judgment rather than established analytics, and its average effect may be close to zero.",
